course: dictionary
lesson: 648
title: Bacteria: tiny living things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bacterium is one tiny living thing made of a single cell. It is so small that you need a microscope to see it. Bacteria is the plural, which means more than one bacterium.
explain: Bacteria live almost everywhere: in soil, in water, in the air and inside our bodies. Some are helpful, like the ones in our gut. Some cause illness, like the ones that cause food poisoning.
explain: Context helps you here. «A virus is much smaller than a bacterium» tells you that a bacterium is bigger than a virus, even if you have never seen either one. Look at the words around a new word and they will give you clues.
fact: A bacterium is one tiny living thing made of a single cell.
fact: «Bacteria» is the plural, which means more than one bacterium.
fact: Bacteria are so small that you need a microscope to see them.
fact: Some bacteria are helpful, like those that help make yoghurt and cheese.
fact: Some bacteria cause illness, so washing hands and cooking food properly help to kill harmful bacteria.
exercise: «A virus is much smaller than a bacterium.» Which one is bigger? | A virus | *A bacterium | They are exactly the same size
exercise: «Some bacteria make poisons that are not destroyed by cooking.» What does this tell us? | Cooking always makes food completely safe | *Cooking does not always make food safe from bacteria | Bacteria are never found in food | Poisons are always sweet
exercise: How many cells does one bacterium have? | None at all | Thousands of cells | *One cell | Two cells
test: What do you need to see a bacterium? | A telescope | *A microscope | A ruler | A magnifying glass that is as big as a house
test: «Bacteria (plural) live almost everywhere.» Where do bacteria live? | Nowhere on Earth | Only inside plants | *In soil, water and air | Only in the sea
exam: «In landfill, rubbish rots without air and bacteria make methane.» What gas do bacteria make in landfill? | *Methane | Oxygen | Nitrogen | Helium
